out of here.

496
00:18:46,586 --> 00:18:48,172
A Bert I. Gordon film,
after all.

497
00:18:48,206 --> 00:18:49,379
See you.

498
00:18:49,413 --> 00:18:50,965
-What a loser!
-Oh, there has to be

499
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,448
a logical explanation for this.

500
00:18:53,482 --> 00:18:57,103
A town of 150 people
just doesn't disappear.

501
00:18:57,137 --> 00:18:58,793
-This one did.

502
00:18:58,827 --> 00:19:00,689
-Yes, but in general,
they don'tdisappear.

503
00:19:00,724 --> 00:19:02,620
That's my point.

504
00:19:02,655 --> 00:19:05,862
♪♪

505
00:19:05,896 --> 00:19:09,862
-Hey, now she's on "The Abbott
and Costello Show" set!

506
00:19:09,896 --> 00:19:12,137
-Oh, man, I should have
taken home-ec or gone

507
00:19:12,172 --> 00:19:14,724
into teaching or nursing,
like all of the other girls.

508
00:19:14,758 --> 00:19:17,655
Gosh, I'm 21,
and I'm still not married.

509
00:19:17,689 --> 00:19:18,724
Yello?

510
00:19:18,758 --> 00:19:21,068
-Chicago mobile
service operator.

511
00:19:21,103 --> 00:19:23,068
-I'd like to place
a person-to-person call,

512
00:19:23,103 --> 00:19:25,068
please, to Mr. Norman Taggart.

513
00:19:25,103 --> 00:19:26,724
-Me being one of the persons,
of course.

514
00:19:26,758 --> 00:19:28,310
-National Wire Service.

515
00:19:28,344 --> 00:19:31,448
The number is Murray Hill 44836.

516
00:19:31,482 --> 00:19:33,172
-Man, I miss those
kind of phone numbers.

517
00:19:33,206 --> 00:19:34,689
-In what city?
-New York.

518
00:19:35,413 --> 00:19:36,689
-[As Frank Nelson]
Eeeeyeeessss?!

519
00:19:36,724 --> 00:19:38,965
-You wind up that
jet plane story already?

520
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:40,551
-I'm not on the jet story.
Norm, listen.

521
00:19:40,586 --> 00:19:42,586
I'm on to something
I think can be real big.

522
00:19:42,620 --> 00:19:44,413
-Me!
-Mm-hmm.

523
00:19:44,448 --> 00:19:47,482
-Oh, he's right-handed.
-Where?

524
00:19:47,517 --> 00:19:49,413
Oh.

525
00:19:49,448 --> 00:19:51,724
-Scribble, scribble, scribble,
scribble as though I'm writing.

526
00:19:51,758 --> 00:19:52,896
-Huh?

527
00:19:52,931 --> 00:19:55,068
What do you mean,
we can't print it?

528
00:19:55,103 --> 00:19:57,689
-I've given my word to hold off
for a while.

529
00:19:57,724 --> 00:19:59,275
Now listen, Norm.
-You're fired.

530
00:19:59,310 --> 00:20:01,689
-A plane flew over Ludlow
last night about midnight,

531
00:20:01,724 --> 00:20:02,965
just about the time
the lid blew off.

532
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,241
Check on it,
and check Washington.

533
00:20:05,275 --> 00:20:08,551
See if they had an atomicinstallation in the Ludlow area.

534
00:20:08,586 --> 00:20:10,413
-Okay.
-Who am I, your stringer?

535
00:20:10,448 --> 00:20:13,068
-Call me back as soon as you
have anything, right?

536
00:20:13,103 --> 00:20:14,103
Goodbye.

537
00:20:14,137 --> 00:20:16,103
-Geez, her guy Friday.

538
00:20:16,137 --> 00:20:18,000
-Cool.

539
00:20:19,448 --> 00:20:20,620
[Engine starts]

540
00:20:20,655 --> 00:20:24,137
-[Imitating exciting music]

541
00:20:24,172 --> 00:20:26,137
-Ooh.

542
00:20:26,172 --> 00:20:28,689
-Hey, we found 150 people!
You were right.

543
00:20:28,724 --> 00:20:30,482
-Sorry, miss. You can't...

544
00:20:30,517 --> 00:20:31,413
Oh, it's you again.

545
00:20:31,448 --> 00:20:32,586
-I know I can't.

546
00:20:32,620 --> 00:20:34,103
I'd like to have
my camera back, please.

547
00:20:34,137 --> 00:20:37,206
Colonel Sturgeon said he was
going to speak to somebody.

548
00:20:37,241 --> 00:20:39,862
-Hey, Corporal? Mathias?

549
00:20:39,896 --> 00:20:43,068
She wants her camera back.

550
00:20:43,103 --> 00:20:44,344
[Phone buzzes]

551
00:20:44,379 --> 00:20:46,620
-I'll get it.
It's probably for me.

552
00:20:46,655 --> 00:20:47,758
Yello?

553
00:20:47,793 --> 00:20:49,793
-Hi. Do you know
[speaks indistinctly] today?

554
00:20:49,827 --> 00:20:51,034
-What's the score?

555
00:20:51,068 --> 00:20:52,965
-Wrong track, baby.

556
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,620
The airlines confirm
a commercial liner over Ludlow

557
00:20:55,655 --> 00:20:58,172
at 12:03 last night.

558
00:20:58,206 --> 00:20:59,827
And there are no atomic
installations,

559
00:20:59,862 --> 00:21:03,103
secret or otherwise,
within 75 miles of Ludlow.

560
00:21:03,137 --> 00:21:05,000
-Well, it was a possibility.

561
00:21:05,034 --> 00:21:07,310
-The only people who've been
playing around with

562
00:21:07,344 --> 00:21:09,379
radioactive materials
in your vicinity

563
00:21:09,413 --> 00:21:11,655
is the U.S. Department
of Agriculture.

564
00:21:11,689 --> 00:21:13,448
-Anything there?
-Department of Agriculture?

565
00:21:13,482 --> 00:21:14,724
-Yeah!
-Yeah, they've got

566
00:21:14,758 --> 00:21:17,586
an experimental project
just outside Paxton.

567
00:21:17,620 --> 00:21:21,034
-U.S. Department of Agriculture,Illinois Experimental Station.

568
00:21:21,068 --> 00:21:22,310
-Mm-hmm.
Okay, I've got that.

569
00:21:22,344 --> 00:21:23,862
I -- I'll keep in touch.
-I got to go.

570
00:21:23,896 --> 00:21:26,241
I've got someone in my office.

571
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,655
-I have a city idea -- Chicago.
I think it might work.

572
00:21:30,689 --> 00:21:31,724
[Laughter]

573
00:21:31,758 --> 00:21:33,241
-Let's get out of here.
-Yeah.

574
00:21:33,275 --> 00:21:35,655
[Tom Servo whistling]

575
00:21:39,068 --> 00:21:42,000
[Doors thud and creak]

576
00:21:50,655 --> 00:21:52,206
-[Whistles]
-Wow, I don't know about

577
00:21:52,241 --> 00:21:53,965
this movie, you guys.
I mean, nothing's happening.

578
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:55,034
-No.
-I think maybe we should

579
00:21:55,068 --> 00:21:56,793
call the Mads.
We can do that, right?

580
00:21:56,827 --> 00:22:00,344
-Well, I guess,
but I wouldn't advise it.

581
00:22:00,379 --> 00:22:03,206
-Yeah, I mean, why get them
involved, huh?

582
00:22:03,241 --> 00:22:04,965
-Well, I mean, I just think
we should call the Mads.

583
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,586
Why don't we call the Mads?
-I don't know.

584
00:22:07,620 --> 00:22:11,793
-♪ My baby takes
the morning train ♪

585
00:22:11,827 --> 00:22:13,758
♪ She works till 9:00

586
00:22:13,793 --> 00:22:15,034
Frank?

587
00:22:15,068 --> 00:22:18,827
I could not stop picking
at that pan of lemon bars.

588
00:22:18,862 --> 00:22:20,965
I ate half the pan!

589
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:22,034
You're so lucky.

590
00:22:22,068 --> 00:22:24,034
You could eat anything
and not worry.

591
00:22:24,068 --> 00:22:25,448
-Oh, pa-shaw.

592
00:22:25,482 --> 00:22:27,896
I'd give anything to have
that complexion of yours.

593
00:22:27,931 --> 00:22:29,827
-Oh, Frank.

594
00:22:29,862 --> 00:22:31,551
-Hey, wow.

595
00:22:31,586 --> 00:22:33,103
Vicki is on!

596
00:22:33,137 --> 00:22:35,793
-[Sighs]

597
00:22:36,551 --> 00:22:37,586
Whew!

598
00:22:40,310 --> 00:22:43,586
You know, Frank, this is exactlywhat I wanted to do today,

599
00:22:43,620 --> 00:22:45,827
just have the whole day
to ourselves.

600
00:22:45,862 --> 00:22:48,448
-I'm declaring this
National Our Day.

601
00:22:48,482 --> 00:22:49,655
[Both chuckle]

602
00:22:49,689 --> 00:22:52,034
Please, do not let me eat
all of this.

603
00:22:52,068 --> 00:22:54,241
-Well, I shouldn't.

604
00:22:54,275 --> 00:22:55,482
[Both chuckle]

605
00:23:00,620 --> 00:23:01,896
Oh, my God, Frank.

606
00:23:01,931 --> 00:23:04,275
Switch on a game.
Switch on a game!

607
00:23:04,310 --> 00:23:06,482
[Stadium crowd cheering]

608
00:23:06,793 --> 00:23:09,275
-Ay-yi-yi-yi-yi-yi.

609
00:23:09,310 --> 00:23:10,448
Wow.

610
00:23:10,482 --> 00:23:13,310
-So, uh, I guess we can call
the Mads, huh?

611
00:23:13,344 --> 00:23:14,310
-Yeah. Yeah.

612
00:23:14,344 --> 00:23:15,758
-But you know what?
-Yeah?

613
00:23:15,793 --> 00:23:17,137
-I don't think I'm going to
do that for a while.

614
00:23:17,172 --> 00:23:19,344
-Unh-unh.
-No.

615
00:23:19,379 --> 00:23:20,724
-We'll be right back.

616
00:23:20,758 --> 00:23:25,034
♪♪

617
00:23:27,896 --> 00:23:29,000
-[Shudders]

618
00:23:29,034 --> 00:23:31,137
-Wow.
-Strange fellers.

619
00:23:31,172 --> 00:23:34,034
-Come on in.
Just working on plutonium.

620
00:23:34,068 --> 00:23:35,724
-Herman Badillo!

621
00:23:37,689 --> 00:23:40,379
-I'm looking for
the Fornstein wedding.

622
00:23:41,551 --> 00:23:44,586
-Tomatoes like bronze.

623
00:23:44,620 --> 00:23:46,034
-Wow, Suddenly Salad.

624
00:23:46,068 --> 00:23:48,931
-Excuse me.

625
00:23:48,965 --> 00:23:50,931
-Man, those are
cherry tomatoes.

626
00:23:50,965 --> 00:23:52,000
-I beg your pardon.

627
00:23:52,034 --> 00:23:53,896
-I never promised you
a rose garden.

628
00:23:57,068 --> 00:23:59,241
-Hey! This is the produce
department, isn't it?

629
00:23:59,275 --> 00:24:00,655
-I beg your pardon.

630
00:24:00,689 --> 00:24:04,000
-We're working overtime
to keep Dom DeLuise fed.

631
00:24:04,034 --> 00:24:07,827
-The more he ignores me,
the more I'm attracted to him.

632
00:24:08,551 --> 00:24:09,655
-Oh!

633
00:24:09,689 --> 00:24:10,931
-Hello.
-I'm Peter Graves.

634
00:24:10,965 --> 00:24:13,862
-Oh, hello.
I -- I spoke to him,

635
00:24:13,896 --> 00:24:15,172
but I guess he didn't hear me.

636
00:24:15,206 --> 00:24:17,137
-Oh.
He's a deaf mute.

637
00:24:17,172 --> 00:24:18,241
-You insensitive little...

638
00:24:18,275 --> 00:24:19,551
-Working with radiation
can be dangerous.

639
00:24:19,586 --> 00:24:21,000
-I smelled that!

640
00:24:21,034 --> 00:24:22,620
-Accident last year cost him
his speech and his hearing.

641
00:24:22,655 --> 00:24:24,034
-And 600 bucks.

642
00:24:24,068 --> 00:24:25,758
-I'm sorry.

643
00:24:25,793 --> 00:24:27,827
I'm looking for
the project director.

644
00:24:27,862 --> 00:24:30,172
-I'm the project director.
My name is Ed Wainwright.

645
00:24:30,206 --> 00:24:32,655
-I'm mute, too.
-What can I do for you?

646
00:24:32,689 --> 00:24:34,724
Oh, excuse me a minute.

647
00:24:35,827 --> 00:24:37,482
-Kind of a vague sign language,
isn't it?

648
00:24:37,517 --> 00:24:39,793
-I'm sorry, Frank.

649
00:24:39,827 --> 00:24:41,517
-Oh, he dropped
his chocolate sampler.

650
00:24:41,551 --> 00:24:44,034
-Nothing to worry about,
just a slight catastrophe.

651
00:24:44,068 --> 00:24:46,413
-Do you have these
catastrophes very often?

652
00:24:46,448 --> 00:24:48,310
-Well, as often as we have
government cover-ups.

653
00:24:48,344 --> 00:24:49,655
-All the time.

654
00:24:49,689 --> 00:24:51,310
It's hard to keep these
little things from getting in.

655
00:24:51,344 --> 00:24:52,827
These are snails.

656
00:24:52,862 --> 00:24:54,793
Last summer,
it was caterpillars,

657
00:24:54,827 --> 00:24:57,275
and after that, it was
grasshoppers by the drove.

658
00:24:57,310 --> 00:24:58,344
-This guy eats anything.

659
00:24:58,379 --> 00:24:59,965
-And just last week,
it was beetles.

660
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:03,000
-Now it's bagel dogs.
-What can I do for you?

661
00:25:03,034 --> 00:25:05,000
-Well, Mr. Wainwright,
I'm Audrey Aimes.

662
00:25:05,034 --> 00:25:07,241
I'm with
National Wire Service.

663
00:25:07,275 --> 00:25:10,034
I suppose you've heard
about what happened in Ludlow.

664
00:25:10,068 --> 00:25:11,241
-Yes.

665
00:25:11,275 --> 00:25:12,482
-I'd like to kiss you full
on the mouth.

666
00:25:12,517 --> 00:25:13,965
-I'm trying to find out
what was responsible,

667
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:15,965
and it occurred to me
that radiation of some sort

668
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:18,172
might have caused
the destruction out there.

669
00:25:18,206 --> 00:25:19,827
-No, I don't think so.
Here.

670
00:25:19,862 --> 00:25:22,655
-Let me show you a giant
crouton we're working on.

671
00:25:22,689 --> 00:25:23,862
-We're the only people
around here

672
00:25:23,896 --> 00:25:24,724
using radioactive materials.

673
00:25:24,758 --> 00:25:26,206
-[Imitating humming]

674
00:25:26,241 --> 00:25:27,931
-Maybe I shouldn't be keeping
my lunch in here.

675
00:25:27,965 --> 00:25:29,896
I don't know.
-And isotopes aren't explosive.

676
00:25:29,931 --> 00:25:31,137
-And they're packed
with vitamins!

677
00:25:31,172 --> 00:25:32,862
-So I'm afraid your answer
isn't here.

678
00:25:32,896 --> 00:25:33,862
-Oh, I see.

679
00:25:33,896 --> 00:25:35,517
-The big brush off.
-Hmm.

680
00:25:35,551 --> 00:25:37,896
-Tell me, is this thing
really a strawberry?

681
00:25:37,931 --> 00:25:39,965
-Yes.
These are tomatoes.

682
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:41,655
-And those are turkey franks.
-This, we hope,

683
00:25:41,689 --> 00:25:43,758
is the future
of the American farmer

684
00:25:43,793 --> 00:25:46,965
and, for that matter,
all farmers everywhere.

685
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:48,482
-Can you eat them?
-No, not yet.

686
00:25:48,517 --> 00:25:50,379
-I don't even like tomatoes.
-But we hope to develop one day

687
00:25:50,413 --> 00:25:53,034
a hybrid that can be eaten.

688
00:25:53,068 --> 00:25:55,448
-How is it there hasn't been
any publicity on this?

689
00:25:55,482 --> 00:25:57,931
-Oh, there have been a few
stories in the farm journals,

690
00:25:57,965 --> 00:26:00,482
but to most of the public,
these giants are just

691
00:26:00,517 --> 00:26:03,310
freaks of nature
with no practical value.

692
00:26:03,344 --> 00:26:06,034
-Ah, get this thing off me!
-But how do they get so big?

693
00:26:06,068 --> 00:26:08,448
-Well, radiation causes
photosynthesis,

694
00:26:08,482 --> 00:26:11,655
that is the growing process,
to continue night and day.

695
00:26:11,689 --> 00:26:14,310
Radioisotopes act as
a sort of artificial sun.

696
00:26:14,344 --> 00:26:15,482
-That's it.
Reel her in, boss.

697
00:26:15,517 --> 00:26:17,931
-Sun that never sets.
-It's fascinating.

698
00:26:17,965 --> 00:26:19,758
Now tell me, what's he doing?

699
00:26:19,793 --> 00:26:21,586
-Well, that's plant food,
essential minerals.

700
00:26:21,620 --> 00:26:22,827
-And he's eating it.

701
00:26:22,862 --> 00:26:24,137
-Keeps the plants from
burning themselves out.

702
00:26:24,172 --> 00:26:26,034
They have to be fed constantly.

703
00:26:26,068 --> 00:26:27,862
Actually, the fruit
would grow much larger

704
00:26:27,896 --> 00:26:29,724
if we didn't limit
the stimulation.

705
00:26:29,758 --> 00:26:31,379
-Speaking of stimulation...

706
00:26:31,413 --> 00:26:33,034
-Maybe you'd like to do
a story on us.

707
00:26:33,068 --> 00:26:34,517
I'd be glad to tell you more
about the work.

708
00:26:34,551 --> 00:26:35,896
-Ooh, schmooze overdrive.
-Thank you very much.

709
00:26:35,931 --> 00:26:37,896
I'd be very interested.

710
00:26:37,931 --> 00:26:40,482
Well, right now I'm working
on this Ludlow story.

711
00:26:40,517 --> 00:26:44,034
-If you could see my hands
right now, you'd be horrified!

712
00:26:44,068 --> 00:26:45,137
-Bye.

713
00:26:45,172 --> 00:26:47,931
-Bye.

714
00:26:47,965 --> 00:26:50,103
-Don't start.

715
00:26:50,137 --> 00:26:51,689
-♪ That's my mother, dear

716
00:26:51,724 --> 00:26:55,793
♪ She helps me
through everything I do ♪

717
00:26:55,827 --> 00:26:58,206
-Three jerks and a Jill.

718
00:26:58,241 --> 00:27:01,000
-Colonel!

719
00:27:01,034 --> 00:27:02,172
-You here?

720
00:27:02,206 --> 00:27:03,482
-Well, they wouldn't
let me inside, so...

721
00:27:03,517 --> 00:27:06,068
-Oh, Colonel, Springfield
is on the line.

722
00:27:06,103 --> 00:27:07,482
-Colonel, you just got back
from Ludlow.

723
00:27:07,517 --> 00:27:09,137
You said after you got back,
I could go.

724
00:27:09,172 --> 00:27:11,137
-I said maybe.
-Well, how about it?

725
00:27:11,172 --> 00:27:12,655
-Tomorrow, it'll be
open to the press.

726
00:27:12,689 --> 00:27:14,896
-Oh, Colonel, be fair!
I played ball with you.

727
00:27:14,931 --> 00:27:17,551
Give me the jump on the other
reporters who'll be in here.

728
00:27:17,586 --> 00:27:19,310
At least let me take
some pictures in Ludlow.

729
00:27:19,344 --> 00:27:21,793
I promise I won't put them
on the wire till tomorrow.

730
00:27:21,827 --> 00:27:23,034
-Well, I guess
you're right that,

731
00:27:23,068 --> 00:27:25,241
for effort anyway.

732
00:27:25,275 --> 00:27:26,655
Barton, take Miss Aimes
to Ludlow.

733
00:27:26,689 --> 00:27:27,793
-Yes, sir!

734
00:27:27,827 --> 00:27:29,275
-And I hope you have
a strong stomach.

735
00:27:29,310 --> 00:27:30,862
-Gabe Kaplan's performing.

736
00:27:30,896 --> 00:27:32,034
-Oh.

737
00:27:32,068 --> 00:27:34,689
-He's nice.

738
00:27:34,724 --> 00:27:36,620
-We're going to take
some pictures in Ludlow.

739
00:27:36,655 --> 00:27:40,034
If we're not back in 15 minutes,better come in after us.

740
00:27:40,068 --> 00:27:42,172
-Check out that guy over there.
He's taken a few in the head.

741
00:27:42,206 --> 00:27:43,724
-Let them through!

742
00:27:46,379 --> 00:27:49,241
-Oh, that wouldn't stop
a kid on a Big Wheel.

743
00:27:49,275 --> 00:27:52,620
-Toughest job you'll
ever love, ugh.

744
00:27:53,241 --> 00:27:55,724
-All right, Captain.
Way to go! Whoo!

745
00:27:58,000 --> 00:27:59,551
-Huh?

746
00:27:59,586 --> 00:28:01,689
Suddenly, it's turned
into "Topper."

747
00:28:03,931 --> 00:28:06,103
-Somebody turn down
the Albert Glasser music.

748
00:28:06,137 --> 00:28:07,517
I can't concentrate.

749
00:28:07,551 --> 00:28:10,137
-I wish the Larsons would take
better care of their yard.

750
00:28:10,172 --> 00:28:12,206
-Oh, it's a disgrace.
-Oh, look at that.

751
00:28:12,241 --> 00:28:14,586
-My goodness!

752
00:28:14,620 --> 00:28:17,517
-Well, want to stop and loot?

753
00:28:17,551 --> 00:28:19,551
-Hey, it's a Star City.

754
00:28:21,344 --> 00:28:24,931
-Oh, look, there's
a perfectly good 2x4.

755
00:28:24,965 --> 00:28:27,000
-This was no boating accident!

756
00:28:30,965 --> 00:28:33,206
-[Imitating dramatic music]

757
00:28:33,241 --> 00:28:36,862
-I've had enough.
-I can't stand reality.

758
00:28:36,896 --> 00:28:38,310
-Whoa, Colonel!
-Some people use calendars

759
00:28:38,344 --> 00:28:40,103
to tell age.

760
00:28:40,137 --> 00:28:42,344
I could use ruins to count mine.

761
00:28:42,379 --> 00:28:43,689
-Oh, am I here, too?

762
00:28:43,724 --> 00:28:46,206
-I was 25 when I went
through Seoul after the shells.

763
00:28:46,241 --> 00:28:47,620
-I'm wearing panties right now.

764
00:28:47,655 --> 00:28:49,931
-I was 20 when I took my camera
into Cologne and Berlin

765
00:28:49,965 --> 00:28:51,172
after World War II.

766
00:28:51,206 --> 00:28:52,862
-24 when I saw Dylan
at the Bitter End.

767
00:28:52,896 --> 00:28:56,448
-Must be used to it by now.

768
00:28:56,482 --> 00:28:58,862
-Captain, there's some things
you never get used to.

769
00:28:58,896 --> 00:29:00,551
-Yeah, like underarm odor.

770
00:29:03,862 --> 00:29:06,586
-Thanks.
How about my kiss?

771
00:29:06,620 --> 00:29:08,965
-How about a drink to wipe away
some memories?

772
00:29:09,000 --> 00:29:10,827
A good way to get rid
of those jitters.

773
00:29:10,862 --> 00:29:11,965
I know a little place --

774
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:14,517
-How do 150 people vanish
into thin air?

775
00:29:14,551 --> 00:29:15,689
-Yeah, terrible.
Martinis?

776
00:29:15,724 --> 00:29:16,965
-Well, around this part
of the country,

777
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:19,172
things seem to have
a way of vanishing.

778
00:29:19,206 --> 00:29:21,413
Only a couple of months ago,
it was a warehouse.

779
00:29:21,448 --> 00:29:23,620
Kind of fell apart overnight,
just like Ludlow.

780
00:29:23,655 --> 00:29:25,103
-Goodbye, Captain.

781
00:29:25,137 --> 00:29:26,310
-Oh, hello?

782
00:29:26,344 --> 00:29:28,793
-Okay. Okay.
You can have your raise.

783
00:29:31,068 --> 00:29:32,586
-Hey.
He saw and hears something.

784
00:29:32,620 --> 00:29:34,965
[Car door closes]

785
00:29:36,931 --> 00:29:39,517
-Women on the verge
of an atomic breakdown.

786
00:29:39,551 --> 00:29:41,586
-She wants me so bad.

787
00:29:44,517 --> 00:29:45,586
-Let's get on with
the harassment

788
00:29:45,620 --> 00:29:46,827
so we can get some work done.

789
00:29:46,862 --> 00:29:48,103
-Hello, Frank.
-Hi.

790
00:29:48,137 --> 00:29:50,275
Did you decide to come back
and do a story on us?

791
00:29:50,310 --> 00:29:51,517
-Well, no.

792
00:29:51,551 --> 00:29:53,275
As a matter of fact,
I came back for some help.

793
00:29:53,310 --> 00:29:55,068
I'd like you to do me a favor.

794
00:29:55,103 --> 00:29:56,344
-Oh, yes.
-Anything I can.

795
00:29:56,379 --> 00:29:57,517
-Does it involve
taking my pants off?

796
00:29:57,551 --> 00:29:58,827
-Remember the warehouse
that was destroyed

797
00:29:58,862 --> 00:30:00,000
about three months ago?

798
00:30:00,034 --> 00:30:01,000
-Uh-huh.

799
00:30:01,034 --> 00:30:02,379
-I want you to take me
to see it.

800
00:30:02,413 --> 00:30:03,931
-Then to dinner and a movie.

801
00:30:03,965 --> 00:30:05,793
-Oh, well, I, uh --
I'd like to,

802
00:30:05,827 --> 00:30:08,206
but I've got too much work
on my hands right now.

803
00:30:08,241 --> 00:30:10,517
How about tomorrow?

804
00:30:10,551 --> 00:30:12,620
-Three months ago,
a warehouse was destroyed,

805
00:30:12,655 --> 00:30:15,689
and the one person
in it vanished.

806
00:30:15,724 --> 00:30:17,862
This morning,
Ludlow was destroyed.

807
00:30:17,896 --> 00:30:19,310
All the people in it vanished.

808
00:30:19,344 --> 00:30:20,655
-Coincidence?
Read the book.

809
00:30:20,689 --> 00:30:22,482
-Don't you think there's
a possible tie-up?

810
00:30:22,517 --> 00:30:24,137
-Look, lay off.
Lay off!

811
00:30:24,172 --> 00:30:27,275
-It's possible, I suppose.
What do you want me to do?

812
00:30:27,310 --> 00:30:30,724
-Just ride out there with me
and take a look at it.

813
00:30:30,758 --> 00:30:32,241
-I don't understand
what good that'll do.

814
00:30:32,275 --> 00:30:34,413
The authorities
investigated it thoroughly.

815
00:30:34,448 --> 00:30:36,068
-Look, I'm coming on to you.
Don't you get it?

816
00:30:36,103 --> 00:30:38,655
-The sheriff thinks in terms
of crime and publicity.

817
00:30:38,689 --> 00:30:40,275
You're a scientist.

818
00:30:40,310 --> 00:30:42,827
You think in terms
of cause and effect.

819
00:30:42,862 --> 00:30:45,965
Maybe you will see something
that the sheriff missed.

820
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:48,000
-[Chuckles] Oh, that shouldn't
be too difficult.

821
00:30:48,034 --> 00:30:49,655
Still, I-I don't know.

822
00:30:52,068 --> 00:30:53,482
-Frank, that's filthy!

823
00:30:53,517 --> 00:30:56,034
I should wash your hands
out with soap.

824
00:30:56,068 --> 00:30:57,586
-What's he saying?

825
00:30:57,620 --> 00:30:58,965
-[Laughs] He says that, uh,

826
00:30:59,000 --> 00:31:00,379
that your lips are easy to read,

827
00:31:00,413 --> 00:31:03,034
that your theory makes you
a very bright girl in his book,

828
00:31:03,068 --> 00:31:04,310
and that he'd like
to go along with us.

829
00:31:04,344 --> 00:31:06,827
-And he's being held here
against his will.

830
00:31:06,862 --> 00:31:08,103
Oops.

831
00:31:08,137 --> 00:31:09,689
-Peter, no!
-No, stop!

832
00:31:09,724 --> 00:31:11,034
-I didn't want to see it.
Whew.

833
00:31:11,068 --> 00:31:13,172
-When you barge into people's
lives and drag them off

834
00:31:13,206 --> 00:31:14,586
to places they don't
really want to go,

835
00:31:14,620 --> 00:31:17,655
aren't you sort of in danger
of becoming unpopular?

836
00:31:17,689 --> 00:31:19,620
-Slug Bug!
-Ow.

837
00:31:19,655 --> 00:31:20,724
-Oh, I thought...

838
00:31:20,758 --> 00:31:22,724
-How'd you pick
such an occupation?

839
00:31:22,758 --> 00:31:24,655
-Wait, this isn't
a stick shift!

840
00:31:24,689 --> 00:31:26,241
Aaah!

841
00:31:26,275 --> 00:31:28,517
-I guess I was just born
inquisitive.

842
00:31:28,551 --> 00:31:30,620
Ever since I can remember,
I wanted to know the why

843
00:31:30,655 --> 00:31:34,551
and where for just
about everything I saw.

844
00:31:34,586 --> 00:31:37,034
I inherited my knack
for the camera from Dad.

845
00:31:37,068 --> 00:31:38,275
-Yourdad, as a matter of fact.

846
00:31:38,310 --> 00:31:40,379
-My curiosity supplied
the nose for news,

847
00:31:40,413 --> 00:31:42,241
and the camera
supplied the memory.

848
00:31:42,275 --> 00:31:44,517
-[Chuckles]
-So there you have it.

849
00:31:44,551 --> 00:31:47,103
-Whatever.
-What about you?

850
00:31:47,137 --> 00:31:49,000
Have you always been
interested in science?

851
00:31:49,034 --> 00:31:50,448
-Well, after my German dance
hall days...

852
00:31:50,482 --> 00:31:53,172
-Oh, I guess I always have been
in one way or another.

853
00:31:53,206 --> 00:31:54,724
I used to fool
around with radios

854
00:31:54,758 --> 00:31:57,172
and anything electrical
when I was a kid.

855
00:31:57,206 --> 00:31:58,862
I was a radar officer
during the war,

856
00:31:58,896 --> 00:32:00,724
and then I went into this
when I got out.

857
00:32:00,758 --> 00:32:03,793
-Look, let's just play
the radio for a while, hm?

858
00:32:03,827 --> 00:32:05,413
-Oh, yes, I remember now

859
00:32:05,448 --> 00:32:07,413
that every time
I read one of your articles,

860
00:32:07,448 --> 00:32:11,413
it was datelined from some area
of flood or famine or war.

861
00:32:11,448 --> 00:32:13,172
Made me realize
what a sheltered life

862
00:32:13,206 --> 00:32:14,931
we scientists really lead.

863
00:32:14,965 --> 00:32:16,206
-What do you think of that?

864
00:32:16,241 --> 00:32:19,620
-Sheltered?
Look what happened to Frank.

865
00:32:19,655 --> 00:32:20,862
-Yeah, watch this.

866
00:32:20,896 --> 00:32:22,517
[Screaming] Hey, Frank!!

867
00:32:22,551 --> 00:32:23,896
[Laughs]

868
00:32:23,931 --> 00:32:30,896
♪♪

869
00:32:36,862 --> 00:32:40,793
-Geez, Frank, you should have
thought of that before we left.

870
00:32:40,827 --> 00:32:42,034
-Doesn't look that great,

871
00:32:42,068 --> 00:32:43,758
but they've got great
onion rings here.

872
00:32:43,793 --> 00:32:46,655
-Mm.
-Oh, yeah.

873
00:32:46,689 --> 00:32:47,551
-Huh?
-What?

874
00:32:47,586 --> 00:32:48,689
-Huh?
-What the...

875
00:32:48,724 --> 00:32:50,068
-What the...
-Hey, this is even worse

876
00:32:50,103 --> 00:32:52,724
than that other stuffthat we didn't know what it was.

877
00:32:56,068 --> 00:32:58,931
-Well, it's a fixer-upper.
That's for sure.

878
00:33:02,655 --> 00:33:05,103
-Looks to me as though
some force had to push

879
00:33:05,137 --> 00:33:06,586
these walls out from the inside?

880
00:33:06,620 --> 00:33:09,275
-Der, hey.
-Think it was an explosion?

881
00:33:09,310 --> 00:33:10,689
-No, it couldn't have been.

882
00:33:10,724 --> 00:33:12,758
Any explosion big enough
to destroy this warehouse

883
00:33:12,793 --> 00:33:14,413
would certainly
have been heard in Ludlow.

884
00:33:14,448 --> 00:33:16,000
-Well, at least
we saved this hat.

885
00:33:16,034 --> 00:33:17,551
-Wonder what they
kept stored here.

886
00:33:17,586 --> 00:33:19,827
-Wheat, almost
a million bushels.

887
00:33:19,862 --> 00:33:22,206
-Wow.
-That's a lot of wheat.

888
00:33:22,241 --> 00:33:24,862
-It was surplus to keep themarket from being over-supplied.

889
00:33:24,896 --> 00:33:26,103
-I hate wheat.
-Does it strike you

890
00:33:26,137 --> 00:33:27,724
as a little strange
that out of all that wheat,

891
00:33:27,758 --> 00:33:29,689
there's not a grain
left anywhere?

892
00:33:29,724 --> 00:33:31,448
-No, that's
nature's way, Audrey.

893
00:33:31,482 --> 00:33:34,413
Birds probably cleaned up
the leavings.

894
00:33:34,448 --> 00:33:35,586
-[As Woody Allen]
Geez, I'm deaf.

895
00:33:35,620 --> 00:33:37,517
They're talking
about wheat over here.

896
00:33:37,551 --> 00:33:40,000
-That's Frank's way of yelling.

897
00:33:42,758 --> 00:33:45,000
-Wha -- wha -- what?

898
00:33:45,034 --> 00:33:47,827
-Wow, that's
a whole lot of skirt.

899
00:33:47,862 --> 00:33:49,448
-I see what you mean, Frank.

900
00:33:49,482 --> 00:33:51,965
-What is it, Ed?
-What -- what did he say?

901
00:33:52,000 --> 00:33:54,275
-You see how barren
this ground is?

902
00:33:54,310 --> 00:33:56,655
-Wow.

903
00:33:56,689 --> 00:33:58,862
-I don't know. I've seen
horses leave it like this.

904
00:33:58,896 --> 00:34:01,758
-Oh, this is deeper than horses
go and much more thorough.

905
00:34:01,793 --> 00:34:02,793
-And much more fun.

906
00:34:02,827 --> 00:34:04,275
-Practically down
to the roots.

907
00:34:04,310 --> 00:34:06,724
The horses pick and choose.
They leave patches.

908
00:34:06,758 --> 00:34:08,172
-I was in "Fury,"
so I know that.

909
00:34:08,206 --> 00:34:10,482
-This is completely barren.
-Please don't say that word.

910
00:34:10,517 --> 00:34:13,586
I'm very sensitive right now.

911
00:34:14,793 --> 00:34:16,620
-Well, leave it to old Frank.

912
00:34:16,655 --> 00:34:19,344
He'll make a botanist
out of me yet.

913
00:34:19,379 --> 00:34:20,551
-You were head
of a government lab,

914
00:34:20,586 --> 00:34:22,172
and you need lessons in botany?

915
00:34:22,206 --> 00:34:24,344
-Well, just lay off!
-I try to teach him what I can

916
00:34:24,379 --> 00:34:26,103
about my field of study.

917
00:34:26,137 --> 00:34:28,275
He tries to teach me
what he can about his.

918
00:34:28,310 --> 00:34:30,206
-Aren't you a botanist?
-No, no.

919
00:34:30,241 --> 00:34:32,655
I'm an entomologist,
the study of insects.

920
00:34:32,689 --> 00:34:33,896
-Oh, fascinating.

921
00:34:33,931 --> 00:34:35,482
-Then how come you're working
with plants?

922
00:34:35,517 --> 00:34:36,758
-Golly, you're right!

923
00:34:36,793 --> 00:34:38,965
-Well, the existence
and development of plants

924
00:34:39,000 --> 00:34:40,862
and insects are
very closely related.

925
00:34:40,896 --> 00:34:43,034
They're highly dependent
on one another.

926
00:34:43,068 --> 00:34:45,379
As a plain matter of fact, onecouldn't live without the other.

927
00:34:45,413 --> 00:34:46,551
-Mm-hmm.
-Bogus!

928
00:34:46,586 --> 00:34:47,896
-That's why
I can't understand.

929
00:34:47,931 --> 00:34:50,931
Ground like this is usually
teeming with insects.

930
00:34:50,965 --> 00:34:52,034
-Yeah.
-This area is

931
00:34:52,068 --> 00:34:53,448
completely devoid of it.

932
00:34:53,482 --> 00:34:55,034
-Hm.
-Do you like Fiddle Faddle?

933
00:34:55,068 --> 00:34:56,655
-I think I'd like to get
some shots of this.

934
00:34:56,689 --> 00:34:58,551
I'm going back to the car
for my camera.

935
00:34:58,586 --> 00:35:01,172
-I'd really like to see you rub
your forearms together.

936
00:35:01,206 --> 00:35:03,172
-Whoa.

937
00:35:05,000 --> 00:35:06,827
-Hey, an Indian Head penny!

938
00:35:06,862 --> 00:35:07,965
-Oh, yeah.

939
00:35:08,000 --> 00:35:09,103
-Here, let me help you.

940
00:35:09,137 --> 00:35:11,344
-[Groans] Oh.

941
00:35:11,379 --> 00:35:12,655
[Chirping]

942
00:35:12,689 --> 00:35:13,931
-What was that?

943
00:35:13,965 --> 00:35:15,586
-I don't know.

944
00:35:17,034 --> 00:35:18,310
-Oh, it might be coming from
behind that

945
00:35:18,344 --> 00:35:21,344
rather large grasshopper.
-I think...

946
00:35:23,275 --> 00:35:25,620
-Oh, luckily the grasshopper
is closed captioned.

947
00:35:25,655 --> 00:35:28,206
-Frank, would you quit clowning
with that giant grasshopper?

948
00:35:28,241 --> 00:35:30,103
-Oh, no! Stop!

949
00:35:31,034 --> 00:35:32,827
-He's signing, "Ah!"

950
00:35:32,862 --> 00:35:35,172
-Oh, no.
-You can't save him.

951
00:35:35,206 --> 00:35:36,586
-Get in the car.

952
00:35:36,620 --> 00:35:38,482
-Quickly, get a really
big applesauce jar

953
00:35:38,517 --> 00:35:41,275
with holes poked in the lid!

954
00:35:41,310 --> 00:35:44,724
-Hey, Diane Arbus,
why don't you take a picture?

955
00:35:44,758 --> 00:35:46,689
You've got a camera.
-We'll send help!

956
00:35:46,724 --> 00:35:49,172
Bye-bye!

957
00:35:50,137 --> 00:35:52,620
-So, feel like Chinese?

958
00:35:52,655 --> 00:35:54,551
-The Geiger counter
showed no radioactivity

959
00:35:54,586 --> 00:35:55,793
to speak of, sir.

960
00:35:55,827 --> 00:35:57,068
Only background.

961
00:35:57,103 --> 00:35:58,793
-There's got to be
an explanation somewhere.

962
00:35:58,827 --> 00:36:00,517
-I've got your explanation
for you, Tom.

963
00:36:00,551 --> 00:36:02,758
-We've got bugs.
-Now, listen.

964
00:36:02,793 --> 00:36:04,206
You've known me ever
since I came to Paxton.

965
00:36:04,241 --> 00:36:05,551
You know I'm not
given to hysteria,

966
00:36:05,586 --> 00:36:07,758
and you've got to listen to me
with an open mind.

967
00:36:07,793 --> 00:36:10,103
-[Shouting gibberish]

968
00:36:10,137 --> 00:36:11,206
-What are you talking about?

969
00:36:11,241 --> 00:36:13,034
-I'm talking about
giant locusts.

970
00:36:13,068 --> 00:36:14,448
-Wow.
-Giant locusts are responsible

971
00:36:14,482 --> 00:36:16,551
for all of this.
-I love it.

972
00:36:16,586 --> 00:36:18,413
-No evidence of any explosion,
Colonel.

973
00:36:18,448 --> 00:36:19,931
The buildings look more like
they were hit

974
00:36:19,965 --> 00:36:21,275
with a battering ram.

975
00:36:21,310 --> 00:36:23,413
We found these guns
at the scene,

976
00:36:23,448 --> 00:36:25,310
the kind people
keep in their home.

977
00:36:25,344 --> 00:36:26,344
They've been fired.

978
00:36:26,379 --> 00:36:27,862
-Can I go home now?
-Lieutenant.

979
00:36:27,896 --> 00:36:29,068
-Now, listen, Tom.

980
00:36:29,103 --> 00:36:30,965
These are 8-feet tall,
some even bigger.

981
00:36:31,000 --> 00:36:32,310
They're vicious,
merciless killers.

982
00:36:32,344 --> 00:36:34,241
-Now, Ed.
-Stops him cold with that,

983
00:36:34,275 --> 00:36:36,586
"Now, Ed," every time.

984
00:36:36,620 --> 00:36:37,896
-Lieutenant!
Phone Springfield again.

985
00:36:37,931 --> 00:36:39,620
Tell them I'm still waiting
for those specialists.

986
00:36:39,655 --> 00:36:41,724
-Yes, sir.
-Frank Johnson is dead.

987
00:36:41,758 --> 00:36:42,931
He was killed not
half an hour ago.

988
