decent wheat around here?

1227
00:46:19,724 --> 00:46:22,827
-I better get the place ready.

1228
00:46:22,862 --> 00:46:24,137
Yeah.

1229
00:46:25,862 --> 00:46:27,551
-America responds to the new

1230
00:46:27,586 --> 00:46:30,034
Robert Urich,
Faye Dunaway sitcom.

1231
00:46:31,793 --> 00:46:34,655
-Al Bundy takes command!

1232
00:46:34,689 --> 00:46:36,310
-Can I, uh, get a ride
with you guys?

1233
00:46:36,344 --> 00:46:38,965
-Just help yourself
to a gun, Peter.

1234
00:46:39,000 --> 00:46:41,482
[Indistinct shouting]

1235
00:46:42,344 --> 00:46:44,000
Come on.

1236
00:46:45,068 --> 00:46:47,931
-♪ John Jacob
Jingleheimer Schmidt ♪

1237
00:46:47,965 --> 00:46:49,000
-Oh, my God.

1238
00:46:49,034 --> 00:46:50,517
-Insects trained by
Rudd Weatherwax.

1239
00:46:50,551 --> 00:46:54,103
-One weekend a month, my ass!

1240
00:46:54,620 --> 00:46:57,931
-Well, that went well.

1241
00:46:57,965 --> 00:46:59,551
-And every available man...

1242
00:46:59,586 --> 00:47:00,965
-I'm throwing a party!

1243
00:47:01,000 --> 00:47:02,344
-Also get some air support.

1244
00:47:02,379 --> 00:47:04,172
We'll bomb them out
of that forest.

1245
00:47:04,206 --> 00:47:05,448
-Yeah, that ought to hold
a little...

1246
00:47:05,482 --> 00:47:06,620
-What are you going to do?

1247
00:47:06,655 --> 00:47:08,206
-Going in there and wipe out
every last one.

1248
00:47:08,241 --> 00:47:09,241
-They'll slaughter you.

1249
00:47:09,275 --> 00:47:10,793
-Not this time.
-Dig this.

1250
00:47:10,827 --> 00:47:12,896
-I want light artillery
brought up with company H.

1251
00:47:12,931 --> 00:47:14,517
-You don't have enough men.

1252
00:47:14,551 --> 00:47:16,758
-There will be three regiments
out there tomorrow by 0300.

1253
00:47:16,793 --> 00:47:18,862
-You don't understand.You still don't have enough men.

1254
00:47:18,896 --> 00:47:20,344
-Not enough men for a couple
of hundred locusts?

1255
00:47:20,379 --> 00:47:21,620
-There are more than
a couple hundred.

1256
00:47:21,655 --> 00:47:22,758
-But you said before --

1257
00:47:22,793 --> 00:47:24,034
-That was before
I heard them screech.

1258
00:47:24,068 --> 00:47:25,620
The noise they made
convinces me there are more.

1259
00:47:25,655 --> 00:47:26,724
-How many are there?
-I don't know.

1260
00:47:26,758 --> 00:47:28,275
There could be
countless numbers.

1261
00:47:28,310 --> 00:47:30,103
I think you better call
in the regular army, Tom.

1262
00:47:30,137 --> 00:47:31,551
-Why would I get off calling
for the regular army

1263
00:47:31,586 --> 00:47:33,206
to handle some
oversized grasshoppers?

1264
00:47:33,241 --> 00:47:35,206
-Boys, boys, don't fight!
-Why, they'd section 8 me

1265
00:47:35,241 --> 00:47:36,103
right out of the service.

1266
00:47:36,137 --> 00:47:37,620
[Telephone rings]

1267
00:47:37,655 --> 00:47:40,310
-Lieutenant, take charge
of the east sector.

1268
00:47:40,344 --> 00:47:41,896
-Aah! What?
-You're making a mistake, Tom.

1269
00:47:41,931 --> 00:47:45,862
-Hi. Anyone for Nutter Butters?

1270
00:47:45,896 --> 00:47:48,896
-Excuse me, bum leg.

1271
00:47:48,931 --> 00:47:51,758
-I'm afraid he doesn't
understand how serious this is.

1272
00:47:51,793 --> 00:47:54,103
-Well, after all, he knows what
that military is capable of.

1273
00:47:54,137 --> 00:47:55,827
-Oh, just jump on me, too!
-That's just it.

1274
00:47:55,862 --> 00:47:57,413
He has faith
in regimental firepower

1275
00:47:57,448 --> 00:47:58,379
because he's seen it work.

1276
00:47:58,413 --> 00:48:00,620
-♪ Faith, faith, faith, baby

1277
00:48:00,655 --> 00:48:02,034
-They've never seen anything
like this before.

1278
00:48:02,068 --> 00:48:03,448
-Well, you've done all you can.

1279
00:48:03,482 --> 00:48:04,758
-No, no, I haven't.

1280
00:48:04,793 --> 00:48:06,034
In a way, I feel responsible,
Audrey.

1281
00:48:06,068 --> 00:48:07,172
-In a way?

1282
00:48:07,206 --> 00:48:08,310
-Everybody's in deadly danger

1283
00:48:08,344 --> 00:48:11,206
if those locusts break
out of the forest.

1284
00:48:11,241 --> 00:48:12,655
-Well, back to the salt mine.

1285
00:48:12,689 --> 00:48:14,172
-What are you going to do?

1286
00:48:14,206 --> 00:48:15,413
-I'm going to Washington.

1287
00:48:15,448 --> 00:48:17,551
Maybe the army people
will listen to me.

1288
00:48:17,586 --> 00:48:18,965
-I'll go with you.
Maybe I can help.

1289
00:48:19,000 --> 00:48:20,275
I saw them, too.

1290
00:48:20,310 --> 00:48:21,793
-All right.
-Mind if a catch a ride?

1291
00:48:21,827 --> 00:48:23,655
-We've got to convince
them, Audrey.

1292
00:48:23,689 --> 00:48:25,344
We may be witnessing
the beginning of an era

1293
00:48:25,379 --> 00:48:28,275
that will mean the complete
annihilation of man.

1294
00:48:28,310 --> 00:48:30,137
-Annihilation?
-What will I wear?

1295
00:48:30,172 --> 00:48:33,620
-Annihilation,
the beginning of the end.

1296
00:48:33,655 --> 00:48:35,862
-Well, real good then.
Bye-bye.

1297
00:48:36,931 --> 00:48:38,586
-And guests at
the beginning of the end stay

1298
00:48:38,620 --> 00:48:40,344
at the beautiful Capitol dome.

1299
00:48:40,379 --> 00:48:44,172
-Inferior guests stay at
the Defense Department Annex.

1300
00:48:45,758 --> 00:48:46,931
-This, gentlemen, is the enemy.

1301
00:48:46,965 --> 00:48:48,275
-Moving pictures.

1302
00:48:48,310 --> 00:48:50,931
-This locust, more commonly
known as the grasshopper,

1303
00:48:50,965 --> 00:48:53,413
is almost identical
to the giant locusts of Ludlow,

1304
00:48:53,448 --> 00:48:55,896
except for its size
and the fact

1305
00:48:55,931 --> 00:48:57,862
that the giants' wings
failed to develop.

1306
00:48:57,896 --> 00:48:59,241
They cannot fly.

1307
00:48:59,275 --> 00:49:00,482
-Now I'll get comfortable.

1308
00:49:00,517 --> 00:49:03,172
-The locust is intelligent,
and it's strong.

1309
00:49:03,206 --> 00:49:05,413
-He's armed.
-The locusts follow a leader.

1310
00:49:05,448 --> 00:49:07,344
-His name is Larry.
-Like the bee and the ant,

1311
00:49:07,379 --> 00:49:09,034
they're able to communicate
with each other.

1312
00:49:09,068 --> 00:49:11,172
-Ant bee?
-This communication, or call,

1313
00:49:11,206 --> 00:49:12,793
is produced by the hind legs.

1314
00:49:12,827 --> 00:49:14,068
-Let me demonstrate.

1315
00:49:14,103 --> 00:49:17,379
-This is the 1956 Australian
locust plague.

1316
00:49:17,413 --> 00:49:19,344
-And this is
the 1910 Fruitgum Company.

1317
00:49:19,379 --> 00:49:22,034
-An area of 400 square miles.
-Seen it!

1318
00:49:22,068 --> 00:49:24,034
-We've been plagued by locusts
since Biblical times.

1319
00:49:24,068 --> 00:49:25,206
-You're not funny!

1320
00:49:25,241 --> 00:49:26,965
-We have various forms
of combating them.

1321
00:49:27,000 --> 00:49:28,551
-Sit down!
-As a matter of fact,

1322
00:49:28,586 --> 00:49:30,103
in our own country,
the early settlers of

1323
00:49:30,137 --> 00:49:31,758
the Massachusetts Bay Colony...
-Ow! Ow!

1324
00:49:31,793 --> 00:49:34,137
-...armed themselves
with bundles of brush

1325
00:49:34,172 --> 00:49:36,344
and drove millions
of locusts into the sea.

1326
00:49:36,379 --> 00:49:39,241
-Neat.
-Now, today, despite the fact

1327
00:49:39,275 --> 00:49:42,000
that we've developed
powerful insecticides...

1328
00:49:42,034 --> 00:49:44,344
-I haven't got a series.
-...the locust still inflicts

1329
00:49:44,379 --> 00:49:46,241
damages to the tune of
$25 million

1330
00:49:46,275 --> 00:49:47,551
in the United States alone.

1331
00:49:47,586 --> 00:49:48,965
-Wow.

1332
00:49:49,000 --> 00:49:51,655
-California, Colorado, Texas.

1333
00:49:51,689 --> 00:49:52,896
-Uh, Connecticut.

1334
00:49:52,931 --> 00:49:54,758
-Even this small locust
will attack a man.

1335
00:49:54,793 --> 00:49:57,068
-Little bastard!
-It has two powerful jaws

1336
00:49:57,103 --> 00:49:58,689
and edged with sharp teeth.

1337
00:49:58,724 --> 00:49:59,896
-And their vicious gases.

1338
00:49:59,931 --> 00:50:01,793
-And they'll kill other insects
and devour them.

1339
00:50:01,827 --> 00:50:03,931
If no other insects
are available,

1340
00:50:03,965 --> 00:50:05,344
it becomes a cannibal...
-Hm.

1341
00:50:05,379 --> 00:50:06,793
-...turning on its own kind.

1342
00:50:06,827 --> 00:50:08,517
-She wants me.

1343
00:50:08,551 --> 00:50:11,862
-What in the good God
are you talking about?

1344
00:50:11,896 --> 00:50:13,172
-Now, you've seen
what the locust can do

1345
00:50:13,206 --> 00:50:16,275
in its normal size,
smaller than your thumb.

1346
00:50:16,310 --> 00:50:18,931
Imagine, if you will, a locust
that's grown bigger than a man

1347
00:50:18,965 --> 00:50:20,896
and is continuing to grow,
some larger than others,

1348
00:50:20,931 --> 00:50:22,172
but each one a deadly killer.

1349
00:50:22,206 --> 00:50:23,448
-I think it will go
something like this.

1350
00:50:23,482 --> 00:50:26,034
-I hope you realize
we haven't much time.

1351
00:50:26,068 --> 00:50:27,793
-You are a scientist,
Mr. Wainwright.

1352
00:50:27,827 --> 00:50:30,137
You know what locusts can do.
I'm a solider.

1353
00:50:30,172 --> 00:50:32,034
I know what guns can do.
-Together we could...

1354
00:50:32,068 --> 00:50:34,310
-I feel secure
the Illinois National Guard

1355
00:50:34,344 --> 00:50:35,586
can handle this situation.

1356
00:50:35,620 --> 00:50:37,275
-Just like they handled
the Chicago Convention.

1357
00:50:37,310 --> 00:50:40,344
-Did you want to say something,
General Hanson?

1358
00:50:40,379 --> 00:50:42,137
-No, sir.
-I'm deeply ashamed.

1359
00:50:42,172 --> 00:50:44,827
-I was greatly impressed with
your presentation, Wainwright.

1360
00:50:44,862 --> 00:50:46,482
I'm sure all of us were.

1361
00:50:46,517 --> 00:50:47,965
Thank you very much for coming.

1362
00:50:48,000 --> 00:50:50,413
-Go in peace, and send no more.
-I'm afraid my presentation

1363
00:50:50,448 --> 00:50:52,586
didn't impress you
quite enough, General.

1364
00:50:52,620 --> 00:50:54,758
-I don't understand you.
-I mean that when the locusts

1365
00:50:54,793 --> 00:50:56,034
start to move out
of that forest,

1366
00:50:56,068 --> 00:50:57,724
I'm not sure you'll be able
to stop them.

1367
00:50:57,758 --> 00:51:00,275
-What are you suggesting
we do, Mr. Wainwright?

1368
00:51:00,310 --> 00:51:02,241
-Hit them with everything
you've got now.

1369
00:51:02,275 --> 00:51:03,551
You need more men,
a lot more men.

1370
00:51:03,586 --> 00:51:05,034
You need tanks
and heavy artillery.

1371
00:51:05,068 --> 00:51:07,896
-As of now, the full strength
of the Illinois National Guard

1372
00:51:07,931 --> 00:51:10,448
is in the lines surrounding
the Ludlow, Illinois, forest.

1373
00:51:10,482 --> 00:51:12,172
-Go away now.
-As I said before,

1374
00:51:12,206 --> 00:51:14,172
Mr. Wainwright,
you are a scientist.

1375
00:51:14,206 --> 00:51:15,793
Why not leave the fighting
to the military?

1376
00:51:15,827 --> 00:51:19,413
-Did you just call me a wussy?

1377
00:51:19,448 --> 00:51:22,482
-Well, I guess I'll just leave
you a few of my brochures.

1378
00:51:22,517 --> 00:51:24,689
-No, that's fine.
Thank you.

1379
00:51:24,724 --> 00:51:25,965
[Phone buzzes]

1380
00:51:26,000 --> 00:51:28,068
-Urgent call from
Paxton, Illinois, sir.

1381
00:51:30,620 --> 00:51:32,551
-Here, you talk
in this end, sir.

1382
00:51:32,586 --> 00:51:33,896
-General Short, here.

1383
00:51:33,931 --> 00:51:37,034
-General Shorthair?
-Oh, I don't know, stuffing!

1384
00:51:37,068 --> 00:51:38,241
Whatever you make.

1385
00:51:38,275 --> 00:51:40,275
-[Muffled] You've got
to help us, General.

1386
00:51:40,310 --> 00:51:41,551
-I see.
-We've got apple juice up

1387
00:51:41,586 --> 00:51:43,310
to our kneecaps.
Oh, please!

1388
00:51:43,344 --> 00:51:44,827
You've got to send them quick.

1389
00:51:44,862 --> 00:51:46,965
-Matt, you will fly to Paxton
with Mr. Wainwright.

1390
00:51:47,000 --> 00:51:48,310
You will take
charge of operations.

1391
00:51:48,344 --> 00:51:49,689
-Hey, I'm vindicated!
How many dead?

1392
00:51:49,724 --> 00:51:53,068
-Mr. Wainwright,
I owe you an apology.

1393
00:51:53,103 --> 00:51:55,241
The locusts have broken
through our defense lines,

1394
00:51:55,275 --> 00:51:57,034
thousands of casualties.

1395
00:51:57,068 --> 00:51:58,448
Our troops are reorganizing

1396
00:51:58,482 --> 00:52:00,517
to prevent Paxton itself
from being overrun.

1397
00:52:00,551 --> 00:52:02,896
-I am trying not to laugh, sir.

1398
00:52:02,931 --> 00:52:05,620
-[Imitating "Indiana Jones"
theme]

1399
00:52:06,689 --> 00:52:09,724
-The hill position in the
suburbs of Paxton must be held.

1400
00:52:09,758 --> 00:52:13,379
Otherwise, the 100 miles betweenPaxton and Chicago lie open.

1401
00:52:13,413 --> 00:52:15,862
-Can I get some nuts?
-General, we can't land

1402
00:52:15,896 --> 00:52:17,827
in Paxton, sir.

1403
00:52:17,862 --> 00:52:18,862
-Why not?

1404
00:52:18,896 --> 00:52:20,758
-It just came
over the radio, sir.

1405
00:52:20,793 --> 00:52:24,000
Giant locusts have
overrun Paxton at 1400.

1406
00:52:26,413 --> 00:52:29,034
-[Muffled] I told you so.

1407
00:52:29,068 --> 00:52:31,034
-Head for Chicago.
-Yes, sir.

1408
00:52:31,068 --> 00:52:34,000
-Well, no more lunches
at Milda's Cafe, huh?

1409
00:52:34,034 --> 00:52:42,586
♪♪

1410
00:52:42,620 --> 00:52:45,275
-"Dear Abby, I'm an elderly
woman who doesn't enjoy s--"

1411
00:52:45,310 --> 00:52:47,206
Oh, this came
to the wrong place!

1412
00:52:47,241 --> 00:52:48,758
-Captain, I want
these positions held.

1413
00:52:48,793 --> 00:52:51,379
I want them held at all costs,
if humanly possible.

1414
00:52:51,413 --> 00:52:53,275
Captain, have your map people
immediately turn out

1415
00:52:53,310 --> 00:52:58,758
300 overlays showing the Chicagodefense line as tentatively set.

1416
00:52:58,793 --> 00:53:00,586
Yes?

1417
00:53:00,620 --> 00:53:02,827
-Thank you,
Sergeant Whisper Thin.

1418
00:53:04,344 --> 00:53:07,517
-Yes, nothing gets tobacco
juice out of your clothes.

1419
00:53:11,448 --> 00:53:14,241
-Boy, I read slow.

1420
00:53:14,275 --> 00:53:15,413
-Major Everett.

1421
00:53:15,448 --> 00:53:16,862
-Our road trip to Peoria
is canceled.

1422
00:53:16,896 --> 00:53:18,000
-What about squadron 12?

1423
00:53:18,034 --> 00:53:19,275
-They haven't reported
in yet, General.

1424
00:53:19,310 --> 00:53:20,482
-Well, let me know
the moment you hear.

1425
00:53:20,517 --> 00:53:21,379
-Yes, sir.

1426
00:53:21,413 --> 00:53:22,724
-I'll be with you in a moment.

1427
00:53:22,758 --> 00:53:24,241
-Okay, son.

1428
00:53:24,275 --> 00:53:26,896
-Hill, you will proceed at once
to temporary GHQ central sector

1429
00:53:26,931 --> 00:53:29,000
and assume command.
-Robert Frost!

1430
00:53:29,034 --> 00:53:30,862
-You will order
the 81st armored division

1431
00:53:30,896 --> 00:53:33,103
to proceed without delay
from Fort Sheridan to Chicago.

1432
00:53:33,137 --> 00:53:34,965
Have the CTU report to me
upon arrival.

1433
00:53:35,000 --> 00:53:37,034
-And get this man an arm.
-Request the Defense Department

1434
00:53:37,068 --> 00:53:39,344
to alert the following
divisions -- the First Armory,

1435
00:53:39,379 --> 00:53:44,103
the 40th, the First Airborne,
the 92nd, and the 76th.

1436
00:53:44,137 --> 00:53:46,620
-Okay, any salad with that?
-Now, what's your problem?

1437
00:53:46,655 --> 00:53:49,965
-At the rate refugees are
pouring into Chicago, General,

1438
00:53:50,000 --> 00:53:52,551
our emergency stocks
won't last long.

1439
00:53:52,586 --> 00:53:54,517
-Then we'll feed them
crickets, yeah.

1440
00:53:54,551 --> 00:53:55,862
-That's it.

1441
00:53:55,896 --> 00:53:58,517
-Ugh.
Now why did I do that?

1442
00:54:01,206 --> 00:54:03,206
-Toxaphene is one
we haven't tried.

1443
00:54:03,241 --> 00:54:04,241
How good is it?

1444
00:54:04,275 --> 00:54:05,551
-Almost as powerful
as chlordane.

1445
00:54:05,586 --> 00:54:06,793
-With no after taste.

1446
00:54:06,827 --> 00:54:08,551
-All right, ship 2,000 gallons
out to the base.

1447
00:54:08,586 --> 00:54:09,965
-Take a pint for yourself.

1448
00:54:10,000 --> 00:54:11,896
[Telephone rings]-Excuse me.

1449
00:54:14,103 --> 00:54:15,655
Hello?
-Bug lab.

1450
00:54:15,689 --> 00:54:17,034
-Speaking.

1451
00:54:17,068 --> 00:54:19,551
-Squadron 12 just reported.

1452
00:54:19,586 --> 00:54:21,758
They say they came down
in the day

1453
00:54:21,793 --> 00:54:24,275
and practically drowned
the enemy in insecticide.

1454
00:54:24,310 --> 00:54:25,482
-I just got a haircut,
and it hurts.

1455
00:54:25,517 --> 00:54:27,275
-The chlordane didn't even
slow them down.

1456
00:54:27,310 --> 00:54:28,758
-They threw it back
at me and laughed.

1457
00:54:28,793 --> 00:54:31,517
-You'll have to come up
with something stronger.

1458
00:54:31,551 --> 00:54:33,551
-I'll do my best, General.

1459
00:54:33,586 --> 00:54:34,862
Goodbye.

1460
00:54:34,896 --> 00:54:36,862
-How about chlordane
with a tiny drop of Retsin?

1461
00:54:36,896 --> 00:54:38,586
-That stuff is no good.
Forget it.

1462
00:54:38,620 --> 00:54:40,689
-It doesn't cause
enough birth defects.

1463
00:54:40,724 --> 00:54:41,965
[Dramatic music plays]

1464
00:54:42,000 --> 00:54:44,000
-Wow.
-Ah, what else can I do

1465
